NAME
       chronyc - check and control the chronyd time service

SYNOPSIS
       chronyc [-n] [-c] COMMAND [ARGUMENT...]

DESCRIPTION
       RHEL keeps its clock right with chronyd, a Network Time Protocol (NTP)
       client and server. chronyd reads /etc/chrony.conf, which names the
       time servers:

           server ntp.lab.example.com iburst
           pool 2.rhel.pool.ntp.org iburst

       server names one machine, pool a name that stands for several.
       iburst sends a few quick requests at start so the clock is set
       sooner. After changing the file, restart the service:

           systemctl restart chronyd

       chronyc asks the running chronyd what it is doing, or tells it to do
       something. Most commands that only show information work for any
       user; commands that change something need root.

COMMANDS
       sources [-v]
              List the time sources. The second character of each line is
              what chronyd thinks of it: * is the source the clock follows
              now, + a good one that is combined with it, - a usable one left
              out, ? one that cannot be reached (yet), x one that seems wrong.
              -v adds a legend explaining the columns.

       sourcestats
              Statistics per source: how much its time drifts and scatters.

       tracking
              How the clock is doing: the reference it follows (Reference
              ID), the stratum, and how far off the system time is.

       makestep
              Jump the clock to the right time now, instead of slewing it
              slowly. Useful after a big correction.

       reload sources
              Read the sources again from the configuration.

       activity
              Count the sources that are online and offline.

       waitsync
              Wait until the clock is synchronised.

OPTIONS
       -n     Print addresses instead of looking up names.

       -c     Print results as comma-separated values, for scripts.

EXAMPLES
       Use the lab's time server and check that it is used:

           echo "server ntp.lab.example.com iburst" >> /etc/chrony.conf
           systemctl restart chronyd
           chronyc sources -v

       See how far off the clock is:

           chronyc tracking

FILES
       /etc/chrony.conf
              The chronyd configuration.

NOTES
       timedatectl shows whether the clock is synchronised ("System clock
       synchronized: yes") and turns NTP on or off with timedatectl set-ntp
       true|false.

SEE ALSO
       chronyd(8), chrony.conf(5), timedatectl(1), date(1)
